  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and seasonable weather continues through the\nweek. The first day of November is the next opportunity for\nrain.\n\nDry weather weather looks to continue through the end of the\nmonth across the region as high pressure remains anchored \nacross the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic states. A shortwave trough\nlooks to flatten the anchored high pressure over the Northeast \ntomorrow, and push it off the coast of Massachusetts. As this \noccurs, a long easterly fetch off the Atlantic through the low \nlevels is likely to lead to a low level cloud deck developing \nover the region early Wednesday morning. Above this low level \ncloud deck, upper level cirrus is also expected to envelope the\nregion from the west thanks to convective blow off from the \naforementioned shortwave trough. All of this extra cloud cover \nwill likely keep temperatures several degrees cooler across the \nregion compared to NBM forecasted highs with wedge conditions \npreventing this cloud deck from eroding until the afternoon \nhours. HREF guidance in addition to other hi-res guidance keeps \ntemperatures several degrees cooler tomorrow compared to NBM, so\nhave blended in these values for tomorrows temperatures. \n\nOverall, as the aforementioned high pressure is replaced by an\nadditional high pressure sliding into the Ohio valley Thursday\ninto Friday, temperatures should rebound each day with cloud\ncover lessening areawide. In addition to clear skies, 500mb \nheights look to increase through the end of the week as upper \nlevel ridging builds across the southeast. This may bring a few \nlocations near 80 degrees on Friday. \n\nAs for the next chance of rain. Models have been consistent with\nadvertisement of a southern stream short wave to cross the\ncountry over the weekend, bringing moisture into the area\nbeginning Sunday November 1st. Some rain may sneak in here late\nHalloween night, but all models still insist on dry conditions\nfor the Trick-or-Treat crowd Saturday evening.\n\nStrength of the initial wave Sunday appears weak, but models do\nindicate southeast upslope flow which should enhance the rain \nchance along the Blue Ridge. A series of disturbances are \nforecast to follow during the week, so it looks promising for \nadditional rainfall, and this should help snuff the late \nOctober dry spell.",
